# Checkpoints v1.1: v1 Metadata at a Custom Ref

**Status:** Design
**Date:** 2026-05-20
**Supersedes:** `2026-05-18-v1-metadata-ref-design.md`. That spec proposed a single-ref relocation auto-enrolled by `entire enable` on fresh repos via an upstream-peek heuristic — existing v1 repos would stay on the branch indefinitely. This design keeps the parts of that spec that were right (custom ref location, tracking-ref namespace, push refactor preserving rebase-on-non-FF recovery, idempotent refspec installation on `entire enable`) and changes two things:

1. **No auto-enrollment.** v1.1 is purely manual opt-in via `strategy_options.checkpoints_version: 1.1` in `.entire/settings.json`. The fresh-setup auto-enrollment, upstream peek, and `applyCustomMetadataRef` helper are all removed.
2. **v1 history preserved for existing repos.** When a user manually flips an existing v1 repo to 1.1, the custom ref is initialized to point at the legacy branch's tip on the first metadata access (a single `SetReference` call). All prior v1 checkpoint history stays reachable from the new ref. No data transformation, no schema change — just one git ref pointer.

## Problem

The manual-commit strategy stores permanent checkpoint metadata on the orphan branch `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1`. Because it lives under `refs/heads/`:

- It shows up in GitHub's branch UI alongside real work branches.
- It is pulled by every `git clone` by default (`refs/heads/*` matches the default fetch refspec).
- Forking, branch-listing, and refactoring tools all see it as a regular work branch.

v2 already moved its refs out of `refs/heads/` and into `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/*` for the same reasons. But v2 has unresolved push-conflict problems because it skipped the mature branch-style fetch-and-rebase recovery flow that v1 uses today.

We want v1's storage format and recovery flow with v2's ref location.

## Goal

Introduce a new value `1.1` for `strategy_options.checkpoints_version` in `.entire/settings.json`. When set, v1 metadata (same format, same trailers, same sharded tree layout, same condense logic, same rebase-on-non-FF recovery) is stored at the custom ref `refs/entire/checkpoints/v1` instead of the branch `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1`.

**Activation is manual opt-in only.** A user writes `checkpoints_version: 1.1` to `.entire/settings.json`. `entire enable` does **not** auto-enroll new repos; setup writes the same v1 (default) settings it does today.

After the manual opt-in:

- On an existing v1 repo with prior checkpoint history, the next metadata access (read or write) preserves the v1 history: a single `SetReference` initializes the custom ref at the legacy branch's tip. Operations proceed at the custom ref. The legacy branch is left intact as a read-only artifact.
- On a fresh repo with no prior history, the custom ref is created as an orphan on the first checkpoint write (same path as v1's orphan creation today; just at a different ref name).
- Running `entire enable` (or re-enable) on a repo where settings already say 1.1 idempotently installs the fetch refspec on origin (`refs/entire/checkpoints/v1` → `refs/entire/remotes/origin/checkpoints/v1`) so subsequent `git fetch` calls update the tracking ref. This handles the teammate-clones-a-1.1-repo case.

Mutually exclusive with v2 — the existing `IsCheckpointsV2Enabled()` selection takes precedence; v1.1 is a sub-mode of v1 (major version 1, minor version 1).

## Non-Goals

- Changing the on-disk metadata format (sharded checkpoint tree, trailers, commit subjects all unchanged).
- Changing v2's storage, push flow, or conflict behavior.
- A subcommand to opt an existing v1 repo into 1.1. (Today: edit `settings.json` and run any Entire command; v1 history is preserved lazily. A friendly subcommand is a follow-up.)
- Deleting the legacy `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1` branch as part of the switch. Cleanup is a future, opt-in operation.
- Backend (entire.io) read-from-custom-ref support — tracked separately.
- Cross-mode interoperability (branch + custom ref simultaneously in a single repo).

## Tradeoff acknowledged: the old branch stays put

The motivation for v1.1 is to stop cluttering `git branch -a` and GitHub's branch UI. Preserving v1 history is conservative — the old branch stays as a read-only artifact, which means existing repos still show the branch in tooling until someone manually deletes it. This is intentional:

- Preserving v1 history must be safe to do automatically without data-loss risk.
- The old branch is still a valid second copy of the same data.
- A future cleanup command can delete the branch once users are confident.
- For brand-new repos that opt into 1.1 *before* their first checkpoint write, no legacy branch is ever created — so they get the full motivation immediately.

The clutter persists for existing repos by design, until an explicit cleanup step is taken.

## Activation rules

`entire enable` does **not** auto-enroll any repo into 1.1. Setup writes v1 (default) settings just like today. v1.1 is opt-in by editing `.entire/settings.json` to set `strategy_options.checkpoints_version: 1.1` (or `"1.1"`).

The only setup-flow change for 1.1 is one idempotent step inside `runEnable` (`setup.go:1102`):

- After flipping `enabled: true`, if the merged settings have `checkpoints_version: 1.1` and the fetch refspec for `refs/entire/checkpoints/v1` is not installed on origin yet, install it.
- This runs on every `entire enable` invocation (re-enable as well as initial setup), so teammates who clone a 1.1 repo and run `entire enable` get the refspec automatically.
- A user who manually edits settings.json on an existing repo can re-run `entire enable` to install the refspec, or accept that initial pushes still work (the push hook's own fetch is explicit and doesn't depend on the refspec) and the refspec is just an ergonomic improvement for manual `git fetch` workflows.

`runSetupFlow` (`setup.go:382`) is **unchanged** by this spec — no upstream peek, no `applyCustomMetadataRef`, no automatic settings rewrite. The fresh-setup path produces v1 (default) settings, exactly as today.

## Settings layer (`cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go`)

### Parser change

`parseCheckpointsVersion(val any) (int, bool)` today recognizes only `1` and `2`. Extend it to also accept `1.1` (as `float64(1.1)` from JSON, or `string("1.1")`):

- Floats: accept `1.0`, `1.1`, `2.0` → return major `1`, `1`, `2` respectively, with `supported=true`.
- Strings: accept `"1"`, `"1.1"`, `"2"`.
- Anything else: `supported=false`, the existing one-shot warning fires.

Return signature stays `(int, bool)` and returns the **major** version. Existing callers (`IsCheckpointsV2Enabled`, the unsupported-version warning, `attach.go:301`, `attach.go:387`) keep working unchanged. `1.1` resolves to major `1`, so `IsCheckpointsV2Enabled()` remains false and the existing `== 2` comparisons are untouched per direction from this brainstorm.

### New helper

```go
// UsesCustomMetadataRef reports whether checkpoints metadata is stored at
// the custom ref (refs/entire/checkpoints/v1) rather than the legacy branch
// (refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1). True iff checkpoints_version is 1.1.
func (s *EntireSettings) UsesCustomMetadataRef() bool {
    if s == nil || s.StrategyOptions == nil {
        return false
    }
    val, ok := s.StrategyOptions["checkpoints_version"]
    if !ok {
        return false
    }
    switch v := val.(type) {
    case float64:
        return v == 1.1
    case string:
        return v == "1.1"
    }
    return false
}

// Package-level convenience wrapper (mirrors IsCheckpointsV2Enabled(ctx)).
func UsesCustomMetadataRef(ctx context.Context) bool {
    s, err := Load(ctx)
    if err != nil {
        return false
    }
    return s.UsesCustomMetadataRef()
}
```

`CheckpointsVersion() int` is unchanged.

## Paths layer (`cmd/entire/cli/paths/paths.go`)

Add one constant alongside the existing v2 ref names. `paths` stays string-only (no go-git import).

```go
// MetadataBranchName is the legacy branch used by v1 storage (under refs/heads/).
const MetadataBranchName = "entire/checkpoints/v1"

// MetadataRefName is the custom ref used by checkpoints_version 1.1 storage.
// Lives under refs/entire/ for the same reasons V2*RefName does: invisible
// in GitHub's branch UI and not fetched by default by `git clone`.
const MetadataRefName = "refs/entire/checkpoints/v1"
```

## Resolver helpers (`cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/metadata_ref.go`)

New file. Holds three helpers: `MetadataRef(ctx)`, `MetadataTrackingRef(ctx)`, and `PreserveV1History(ctx, repo)` (defined in the section below). The resolver lives in `checkpoint/` (not `paths/`) so it can import `settings/` without creating an import cycle and so callers don't have to thread a bool through every call.

```go
package checkpoint

import (
    "context"

    "github.com/go-git/go-git/v6/plumbing"

    "github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/paths"
    "github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/settings"
)

// MetadataRef returns the plumbing.ReferenceName for v1 metadata storage,
// resolved from settings. Legacy v1 repos return the branch ref
// (refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1); checkpoints_version 1.1 repos return
// the custom ref (refs/entire/checkpoints/v1). Falls back to the legacy
// branch ref when settings cannot be loaded.
func MetadataRef(ctx context.Context) plumbing.ReferenceName {
    if settings.UsesCustomMetadataRef(ctx) {
        return plumbing.ReferenceName(paths.MetadataRefName)
    }
    return plumbing.NewBranchReferenceName(paths.MetadataBranchName)
}

// MetadataTrackingRef returns the plumbing.ReferenceName for the
// remote-tracking counterpart of the v1 metadata ref. Used by the push
// hook's divergence-detection logic.
//
// For legacy v1: refs/remotes/origin/entire/checkpoints/v1.
// For 1.1: refs/entire/remotes/origin/checkpoints/v1.
//
// The 1.1 tracking ref is intentionally NOT the same as the local ref —
// mapping a fetched ref to itself would clobber local writes on every
// fetch. The separate namespace preserves local commits the way standard
// refs/heads/* ↔ refs/remotes/origin/* does for branches.
func MetadataTrackingRef(ctx context.Context) plumbing.ReferenceName {
    if settings.UsesCustomMetadataRef(ctx) {
        return plumbing.ReferenceName("refs/entire/remotes/origin/checkpoints/v1")
    }
    return plumbing.NewRemoteReferenceName("origin", paths.MetadataBranchName)
}
```

## Preserving v1 history on opt-in (NEW vs prior spec)

When a repo flips `checkpoints_version` to `1.1`, the legacy v1 branch may already exist with prior checkpoint history. We want that history to remain accessible from the custom ref so commands like `entire status`, `entire explain`, and rewind don't appear to lose data. The mechanism is trivial: one `SetReference` call points the custom ref at the legacy branch's tip.

This preservation step is factored into a dedicated function so that **both read and write paths** trigger it. A read-only command run before any write (e.g. `entire status` immediately after flipping the setting) would otherwise see an empty custom ref and an inaccessible legacy branch.

### `PreserveV1History` (new function in `cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/metadata_ref.go`)

Lives in `checkpoint/` (not `strategy/`) because `strategy/` already imports `checkpoint/`. Putting it in `strategy/` and calling it from `checkpoint/committed.go` would form an import cycle.

```go
// PreserveV1History initializes the custom ref at the legacy v1 branch's
// tip so prior checkpoint history remains reachable under 1.1. Runs at
// most once per repo (idempotent; safe to call repeatedly).
//
// Returns nil and is a no-op when:
//   - The repo is not configured for v1.1.
//   - The custom ref already exists (regardless of legacy branch state).
//   - Neither ref exists (no v1 history to preserve; fresh-orphan creation
//     is the caller's responsibility via EnsureMetadataBranch).
//
// Does NOT create an orphan ref when neither exists — that is reserved for
// EnsureMetadataBranch, which is only called from write paths.
func PreserveV1History(ctx context.Context, repo *git.Repository) error
```

Algorithm:

1. If `settings.UsesCustomMetadataRef(ctx)` is false → return nil. Nothing to do.
2. Resolve `targetRef := checkpoint.MetadataRef(ctx)` (`refs/entire/checkpoints/v1`).
3. If `repo.Reference(targetRef, false)` returns a valid hash → return nil. History has already been preserved (or the repo started fresh on 1.1).
4. If targetRef is missing, check the legacy branch: `repo.Reference(plumbing.NewBranchReferenceName(paths.MetadataBranchName), false)`.
5. If the legacy branch exists with a valid hash → **preserve it**: `repo.Storer.SetReference(plumbing.NewHashReference(targetRef, legacyHash))`. Log at INFO level: `preserved v1 history at custom ref` with `legacy_hash` and `target_ref` attributes (no content logging). Return nil.
6. If the legacy branch is also missing → return nil. The read or write that called us will see an empty/missing ref and handle it.

### Trigger points

- **Write paths**: `EnsureMetadataBranch(ctx, repo)` in `strategy/common.go` calls `checkpoint.PreserveV1History(ctx, repo)` as its first step, before the existing orphan-creation logic. If history was preserved (custom ref now points at the legacy tip), the rest of `EnsureMetadataBranch` short-circuits on "ref already exists". If there was no legacy branch, the existing fresh-orphan path runs.
- **Read paths**: each read entry point in `checkpoint/committed.go` (`ReadCommitted`, `ListCommitted`, `WriteCommitted`'s read-modify-write path, etc.) calls `checkpoint.PreserveV1History` immediately after opening the repo. Errors are logged at WARN and swallowed — a failure falls through to the existing "ref not found" handling, so the read returns the same empty result it would today on a freshly-enabled 1.1 repo.

Read-path callers don't need to thread the call through every helper — only the ~3 entry points in `committed.go` plus `parse_tree.go:447`'s entry point need it.

### Properties

- **Idempotent.** Step 3 short-circuits after the first run, so re-calling is free.
- **Local-only.** The legacy branch is read; nothing is written to it. The legacy ref's target is unchanged.
- **No data loss.** The custom ref starts at the same commit as the legacy branch, so all sharded checkpoint metadata is reachable from the new ref. Subsequent commits accumulate on the new ref.
- **Compatible with concurrent sessions.** `EnsureMetadataBranch` is called from inside the same write paths today; the existing concurrency story (one writer at a time via short-lived go-git transactions) is unchanged.
- **Falls back safely.** If reading the legacy branch errors (corrupted ref, permission issue), the error is logged at WARN and `PreserveV1History` returns nil. On a write path the existing fresh-orphan creation runs; on a read path the read returns empty. The legacy branch is left untouched.

### What this step does NOT do

- It does not delete the legacy branch.
- It does not push the custom ref to origin (that happens on the next normal push via `pushRefIfNeeded`).
- It does not modify remote refs.
- It does not copy or transform the on-disk metadata trees — they are reachable directly from the legacy hash, so a single `SetReference` is sufficient.

### Multi-machine semantics

When user Alice flips `checkpoints_version` to `1.1` in `.entire/settings.json` and pushes the settings change, Alice's next metadata access preserves v1 history on Alice's machine. Bob pulls Alice's settings.json; Bob's next access preserves v1 history on Bob's machine (his legacy branch points at his own pre-pull HEAD; the same `SetReference` produces a valid custom ref locally). Both machines then push to the custom ref. The shared rebase-on-non-FF recovery flow merges any divergence the same way it does today for the branch.

If a machine has the legacy branch but is OUT OF SYNC with origin (e.g., it never pushed its latest v1 commits), the local hash is used — those commits remain reachable from the custom ref locally, and the rebase-on-push flow either pushes them or rebases them on top of origin's custom-ref state. This is identical to today's behavior for the branch.

### Edge cases

| Situation | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Legacy branch exists, target ref exists, both at the same hash | Step 3 short-circuits on the existing target ref. No-op. |
| Legacy branch exists, target ref exists, at DIFFERENT hashes | Step 3 short-circuits. The target ref wins — the legacy branch is treated as the read-only artifact. No automatic reconciliation. |
| Legacy branch doesn't exist, target ref exists | Step 3 short-circuits. Normal v1.1 operation. |
| Legacy branch doesn't exist, target ref doesn't exist | `PreserveV1History` returns nil. If called from a write path, the surrounding `EnsureMetadataBranch` then creates the target as a fresh orphan. If called from a read path, the read sees a missing ref and returns empty. |
| Legacy branch exists with garbage data (unreadable tree) | Preserve the hash anyway — it is just an object reference. If the underlying tree is corrupt, the next read attempt fails normally and surfaces the error. We don't validate object content. |
| Repo has both a legacy branch on `refs/heads/` AND a stale `refs/entire/checkpoints/v1` from a prior failed attempt | Step 3 short-circuits on the existing target ref. The user can manually reset the target ref if they want preservation to re-run; this is an unusual recovery scenario, not a flow we automate. |
| Settings load fails | `UsesCustomMetadataRef` returns false; `PreserveV1History` returns nil (step 1 short-circuits). Behavior degrades safely to legacy v1. |

## Call-site updates

Every site that today builds the v1 metadata reference inline becomes a call to `checkpoint.MetadataRef(ctx)` or `checkpoint.MetadataTrackingRef(ctx)`. Inventory (from the 2026-05-18 spec, unchanged):

| File | Today | Change |
