| `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/cleanup.go:75` (comment) | "The 'entire/checkpoints/v1' branch is NOT a shadow branch" | Update comment: on 1.1 repos the metadata lives at a custom ref entirely outside `refs/heads/`. |
| Integration test helpers in `cmd/entire/cli/integration_test/testenv.go` | Branch-based reads | Stay branch-based for legacy v1 tests; new 1.1 tests use `paths.MetadataRefName` directly. |

## Setup flow (`cmd/entire/cli/setup.go`)

### `runSetupFlow` (existing, line 382)

**Unchanged.** No fresh-setup auto-enrollment, no upstream peek, no settings rewrite. Setup writes v1 (default) settings exactly as today.

### `runEnable` (existing, line 1102)

Adds one idempotent step after the existing `enabled: true` flip:

```go
func runEnable(ctx context.Context, w io.Writer, useProjectSettings bool) error {
    s, err := LoadEntireSettings(ctx)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("failed to load settings: %w", err)
    }
    s.Enabled = true
    if err := saveEnabledState(ctx, s, useProjectSettings); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    fmt.Fprintln(w, "Entire is now enabled.")
    printEnabledStatus(ctx, w)

    if s.UsesCustomMetadataRef() {
        if err := installMetadataRefspec(ctx); err != nil {
            fmt.Fprintf(w, "Warning: failed to install fetch refspec: %v\n", err)
        }
    }
    return nil
}
```

### New helper

```go
// installMetadataRefspec appends a fetch refspec on origin so subsequent
// `git fetch` calls pull refs/entire/checkpoints/v1 into the non-clobbering
// remote-tracking ref. Idempotent. No-op if origin doesn't exist or the
// refspec is already present.
func installMetadataRefspec(ctx context.Context) error {
    if err := exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "remote", "get-url", "origin").Run(); err != nil {
        return nil // no origin
    }
    refspec := "+" + paths.MetadataRefName + ":refs/entire/remotes/origin/checkpoints/v1"
    out, _ := exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "config", "--get-all", "remote.origin.fetch").Output()
    for _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), "\n") {
        if line == refspec {
            return nil
        }
    }
    return exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "config", "--add", "remote.origin.fetch", refspec).Run()
}
```

## Push hook refactor (`cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common.go`)

The current `pushBranchIfNeeded(ctx, target, branchName string)` hard-codes branch refs throughout (`plumbing.NewBranchReferenceName`, `refs/heads/...` in fetch refspecs, `refs/remotes/<remote>/...` for tracking). It is called from one production site: `manual_commit_push.go:36`. Its internal helpers (`hasUnpushedSessionsCommon`, `tryPushSessionsCommon`, `fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon`) are not called from anywhere else.

Refactor:

```go
// New signature, replaces pushBranchIfNeeded.
func pushRefIfNeeded(
    ctx context.Context,
    target string,
    localRef, trackingRef plumbing.ReferenceName,
) error
```

Internal helpers take ref-name strings instead of branch names. The fetch refspec built inside `fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon` is constructed from the passed-in refs rather than `refs/heads/<name>:refs/remotes/<remote>/<name>`. The rebase-on-non-FF flow (collect-local-only-commits → cherry-pick-onto-remote-tip → push merged result) is unchanged in behavior; only the ref names it operates on become parameterized.

Call site in `manual_commit_push.go`:

```go
err = pushRefIfNeeded(ctx, ps.pushTarget(),
    checkpoint.MetadataRef(ctx),
    checkpoint.MetadataTrackingRef(ctx))
```

Behavior:

- Legacy v1 repo: identical to today.
- 1.1 repo: same rebase-on-non-FF recovery flow at the custom ref pair.

This is the central reason 1.1 must not regress: it inherits today's mature branch-style rebase recovery rather than re-implementing it (which is what produced v2's push-conflict problems).

## Other commands and edge cases

### Commands that read the metadata ref

`entire doctor`, `entire resume`, `entire attach`, `entire clean`, `entire status`, `entire explain`, `entire search`, `entire trail` all read through `checkpoint/committed.go`, which uses the resolver after the call-site updates. No additional per-command changes beyond the inventory above.

### Shadow branches

Unchanged. Shadow branches (`entire/<commit[:7]>-<worktree[:6]>`) stay under `refs/heads/`. The orphan branch (today's metadata) is the only thing moving to a custom ref.

### Empty repo

`entire enable` on a repo with no commits works the same as today (v1 default settings). If a user then manually flips to 1.1, the custom ref is created on the first checkpoint write.

### No `origin` remote

`installMetadataRefspec` no-ops when `git remote get-url origin` fails. Pushes are no-ops (no target). Local-only operation works. v1 history is preserved on first metadata access if a legacy branch exists locally.

### Stale `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1` on a 1.1 repo after preservation

By design. The legacy branch is the read-only artifact. Entire commands read only from the resolved ref; the stale branch is unreachable from v1.1 operations. A future cleanup command can prune it.

### `checkpoints_version: 1.1` in `.entire/settings.local.json` only

The merged settings view returns 1.1 — local user's strategy uses the custom ref while the project says branch. Per-machine divergence. Not supported. Document: "Set `checkpoints_version` in `.entire/settings.json`, not in `settings.local.json`."

### Manual edit of project `settings.json` to add `checkpoints_version: 1.1` on an existing v1 repo

This is the primary activation path. Strategy starts using the custom ref. The next metadata access (read OR write) calls `PreserveV1History`, which copies the legacy branch tip to the custom ref so prior history stays reachable. The legacy branch is left untouched. Bob pulling Alice's settings.json change has the same trigger on Bob's first read or write.

### Legacy branch exists, custom ref exists, hashes diverge

`PreserveV1History` short-circuits (custom ref already populated). The custom ref is authoritative. The legacy branch sits stale at its older hash. No automatic reconciliation — the user would need to manually delete the custom ref to force preservation to re-run. Documented as an unusual recovery scenario, not a supported operation.

## Error handling

| Failure | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `installMetadataRefspec` fails (config write error) | Warn to stderr; `entire enable` completes. The custom ref still works; the refspec just makes shared clones' `git fetch` calls more useful and is recoverable by re-running `entire enable`. |
| `PreserveV1History`'s `SetReference` fails (storer error) | Error returned from `PreserveV1History`. On write paths the surrounding `EnsureMetadataBranch` propagates the error and the write fails normally. On read paths the error is logged at WARN and swallowed; the read returns the same empty result it would on a fresh repo. |
| Reading the legacy branch hash fails | Logged at WARN inside `PreserveV1History`. Returns nil. On write paths, the subsequent fresh-orphan creation runs. On read paths, the read sees a missing ref and returns empty. The legacy branch is left untouched. |
| Push to custom ref hits non-FF | Existing branch-path recovery (`fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon`) executes against the custom ref pair via the refactored `pushRefIfNeeded`. Same machinery, same outcome as today's branch path. |
| Push to custom ref hits protected-ref rejection | Existing `printProtectedRefBlock` flow, same as branches. |

## Testing

### Settings package (`settings_test.go`)

- `TestParseCheckpointsVersion_OneDotOne` — table cases for `float64(1.1)` and `string("1.1")` returning major `1` and `supported=true`.
- `TestUsesCustomMetadataRef` — true for `1.1`; false for `1`, `2`, missing, `"junk"`, bool, `1.2`. Covers both the method on `*EntireSettings` and the package-level wrapper.
- `TestCheckpointsVersion_OneDotOneMapsToMajorOne` — confirms `CheckpointsVersion()` returns `1` and `IsCheckpointsV2Enabled()` stays false.
- `TestParseCheckpointsVersion_UnknownVersionWarning` — existing warning still fires for `1.5`, `3`, etc.; 1.1 doesn't open the door to silent acceptance of arbitrary floats.

### Checkpoint package (new `metadata_ref_test.go`)

- `TestMetadataRef_LegacyVsCustom` — fake settings file with `checkpoints_version: 1.1` returns `refs/entire/checkpoints/v1`; without it, returns `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1`.
- `TestMetadataTrackingRef_LegacyVsCustom` — analogous for the tracking-ref helper.
- `TestMetadataRef_LoadFailureFallsBackToLegacy` — when settings fail to load, return the branch ref.

### v1 history preservation (new `metadata_ref_preserve_test.go` in checkpoint/)

- `TestPreserveV1History_CopiesLegacyTipOnFirstAccess` — pre-populate `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1` with checkpoints, flip settings to 1.1, call `PreserveV1History`, assert `refs/entire/checkpoints/v1` now exists at the same hash, legacy branch unchanged.
- `TestPreserveV1History_NoopWhenCustomRefAlreadyExists` — pre-populate both refs; assert it short-circuits and neither ref is rewritten.
- `TestPreserveV1History_DivergedRefs_CustomRefWins` — both refs at different hashes; assert it skips and the custom ref is authoritative.
- `TestPreserveV1History_NoLegacyBranch_NoOp` — no legacy branch, no custom ref; assert no ref is created (orphan creation is reserved for write paths).
- `TestPreserveV1History_V1Mode_NoOp` — confirm it doesn't trigger when settings say v1.
- `TestEnsureMetadataBranch_CallsPreserveV1HistoryFirst` — write-path entry point calls `PreserveV1History`, then short-circuits on the (now existing) custom ref instead of creating a fresh orphan.
- `TestEnsureMetadataBranch_FreshRepoV1_1_CreatesOrphan` — no legacy branch, no custom ref; `EnsureMetadataBranch` creates the custom ref as a fresh orphan.
- `TestReadEntryPoints_CallPreserveV1History` — `ListCommitted` / `ReadCommitted` on a freshly-flipped 1.1 repo (legacy branch present, custom ref missing, no prior write) successfully reads checkpoint data via in-line preservation.

### Push refactor (`push_common_test.go`)

- `TestPushRefIfNeeded_BranchPathStillWorks` — preserve coverage of the existing branch-based push.
- `TestPushRefIfNeeded_CustomRefPath` — same scenarios at the 1.1 ref pair: no-op when local matches tracking, push when ahead, rebase recovery on non-FF.
- `TestFetchAndRebaseSessions_CustomRef` — cherry-pick-onto-remote works correctly at the custom ref pair.

### Setup flow (new `setup_v1_metadata_ref_test.go`)

- `TestRunSetupFlow_FreshRepo_DoesNotWriteV1MetadataRef` — fresh temp repo, in-process setup leaves `checkpoints_version` unset; manual opt-in only.
- `TestRunEnable_OnV1MetadataRefRepo_InstallsFetchRefspec` — settings already have 1.1, refspec missing; `entire enable` installs the refspec.
- `TestRunEnable_OnV1MetadataRefRepo_RefspecInstallIsIdempotent` — settings already have 1.1, refspec already present; `entire enable` is a no-op on the refspec; running it again does not duplicate.
- `TestRunEnable_OnV1Repo_DoesNotTouchRefspec` — settings don't have 1.1; `entire enable` does not modify `remote.origin.fetch`.
- `TestInstallMetadataRefspec_NoOrigin_NoOp` — no remote configured; helper returns nil without touching config.

### Integration tests (new `v1_metadata_ref_workflow_test.go`)

Per CLAUDE.md, use `testutil.InitRepo` and isolated temp repos.

- `TestV1MetadataRef_FreshRepo_ManualOptIn_FullWorkflow` — `testutil.InitRepo` → in-process setup (writes default v1 settings) → manually rewrite settings.json to add `checkpoints_version: 1.1` → re-run `entire enable` so the refspec is installed → run a session with the Vogon canary agent → commit → metadata commit lands in `refs/entire/checkpoints/v1`, NOT in `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1`. Sharded tree layout, `Checkpoint: <id>` subject, trailers intact.
- `TestV1MetadataRef_ExistingV1Repo_PreservesHistoryOnFlip` — set up a v1 repo, accumulate checkpoints on the legacy branch, manually flip settings.json to 1.1, run another session + commit, assert the custom ref now exists at (or beyond) the legacy branch's old hash, legacy branch unchanged at the pre-flip hash.
- `TestV1MetadataRef_ExistingV1Repo_PreservesHistoryOnReadBeforeWrite` — set up a v1 repo with checkpoints, flip settings.json to 1.1, run a read-only command (e.g. `entire status`) before any new write, assert the custom ref now exists at the legacy branch hash and the read returns the prior checkpoints.
- `TestV1MetadataRef_RewindAndResume` — full rewind + resume round-trip on 1.1.
- `TestV1MetadataRef_PushAndFetch_NonFF` — bare remote; push twice from two pseudo-machines with diverging local state; rebase-on-non-FF recovery completes; both sides converge.
- `TestV1MetadataRef_TeammateCloneAdoption` — set up a 1.1 repo, push, simulate a clone, run `entire enable` on the clone; refspec install runs on re-enable; fetch pulls history into the tracking ref.
- `TestLegacyV1_StillWorks_Regression` — run an existing branch-based workflow; no behavior change.

### E2E canary

`mise run test:e2e:canary` should pick up a 1.1 scenario via Vogon (no real-agent API calls). If existing test prompt wording is reused, no Vogon regex updates needed; new prompts may require updating `e2e/vogon/main.go`.

### Test parallelization

All tests use `t.Parallel()` per CLAUDE.md. Setup-flow tests that require `t.Chdir()` either accept serialization or drive the real binary from subprocesses via `execx.NonInteractive` to avoid changing the parent's CWD.

## Documentation

- `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` strategy sections gain a short note: "When `checkpoints_version: 1.1` is set in `.entire/settings.json`, metadata is stored at `refs/entire/checkpoints/v1` instead of the branch `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1`. Same format and trailers; only the ref location differs. Activation is manual opt-in: edit settings.json to set `checkpoints_version` to `1.1`. On existing v1 repos, prior checkpoint history is preserved by initializing the custom ref at the legacy branch's tip on the first metadata access (read or write). Re-run `entire enable` after the edit to install the fetch refspec on origin."
- No new top-level doc page. The change is a config knob, not a new strategy.

## Open follow-ups (out of scope for this change)

- A friendly subcommand to opt an existing v1 repo into 1.1 (today: edit settings.json and run any Entire command; the subcommand would make it discoverable and add a confirmation prompt).
- A cleanup subcommand to delete the legacy branch once a user is confident in their 1.1 history.
- Backend (entire.io) read-from-custom-ref support.
- v2 push-conflict fixes (separate work — v2 should likely adopt the same `pushRefIfNeeded` refactor for the same robustness benefit).
- Cleaning up the `temporary.go:239` branch-name guard once no legacy repos remain in the population (long-tail).

